## Below the water: what people experience

### P: Patterns

#### P1 · Colour and contrast

- [ ] **Text contrast is at least 4.5:1 (3:1 for large text)**  
  Large text means 18pt or above, or 14pt bold. Check pairs with the [PBIX A11y](https://pbiaudits.com) Colour Contrast Checker.
- [ ] **Meaningful graphics reach at least 3:1 against background and neighbouring colours**  
  This covers lines, bars, icons and slicer borders. Pale pastels on a white background are the most common failure.
- [ ] **Information isn't shown by colour alone**  
  Add data labels, direct legend labels, icons or patterns. A quick test: print the page in greyscale. Can we still read it?

#### P2 · Typography and readability

- [ ] **Font size is legible (e.g. titles 28–32pt, body 14–16pt)**  
  We can use 28–32pt for titles, 14–16pt for body text and 12–14pt for labels. Increase font size proportionally to your canvas. Use the [PBIX A11y](https://pbiaudits.com) Minimum Font Size Calculator.
- [ ] **Text is readable on the smallest screen people use**  
  Open the report on a 13" laptop at 100% zoom, not just on a large monitor. If we have to lean in to read it, so will our users.
- [ ] **Labels stay readable when someone zooms in**  
  Zoom in with Ctrl + Plus in the Power BI service. Check that axis labels don't get cut off or turn into "…".
- [ ] **Fonts, icons and title styles match across all pages**  
  Stick to one font family and no more than three text sizes per page. Set title styles in the theme so every visual matches.
- [ ] **Wording is plain and jargon-free**  
  Use the words your users use, not database or team jargon. Rename fields like "Rev_Act_YTD" and spell out acronyms the first time.
- [ ] **Claude dashboards: content reflows at 200% zoom and phone width**  
  Zoom the browser to 200% and narrow the window to about 320px wide. There should be no sideways scrolling, overlapping text or hidden content.

#### P3 · Design systems

- [ ] **Standards for colour, typography and naming are written down**  
  One page is enough to start: palette with contrast ratios, font sizes, title style and naming rules. Store it where the whole team can find it.
- [ ] **Exceptions to the standards are deliberate and recorded**  
  If a report needs a different palette, write down why. Undocumented exceptions slowly become the new, accidental standard.
- [ ] **Claude dashboards: Claude was given our standards**  
  Paste the palette, fonts and accessibility rules into the prompt, or save them as reusable instructions so every dashboard starts from the same base.

### T: Trust

#### T1 · Hierarchy

- [ ] **The most important information is seen first**  
  People usually scan from the top left. Put the key KPI or main message there, and supporting detail below or to the right.
- [ ] **The main question can be answered without jumping around**  
  Ask a colleague to find one specific answer and watch where their eyes and mouse go. Long journeys across the page point to a hierarchy problem.
- [ ] **The reading order makes sense for keyboard users**  
  Press Tab through the page. The focus should follow the same order as the visual layout, usually left to right and top to bottom.
- [ ] **Claude dashboards: the most important content still comes first on a small screen**  
  Narrow the browser window and see how the cards stack. Ask Claude to set the order explicitly if the key KPI ends up at the bottom.

#### T2 · Chart honesty

- [ ] **Axes start at sensible values**  
  Bar charts should almost always start at zero. If a line chart's axis doesn't start at zero, make that obvious so small changes don't look huge.
- [ ] **Time periods, filters and missing data are clear**  
  Show the date range and active filters in a subtitle, e.g. "Jan–Mar 2025, Project P01". Say so when data is incomplete.
- [ ] **AI-chosen calculations and headline numbers have been checked**  
  Pick two or three numbers and recalculate them from the source data. Check that sums, averages and distinct counts are the ones we meant.
- [ ] **AI-written titles, summaries and insights match the data**  
  Once AI starts interpreting the data, review what it says, not just that it exists. Check the direction of every change it describes.

#### T3 · Assistive technology

- [ ] **Every meaningful visual has alt text**  
  Use dynamic alt text: in Format > General > Alt text, click the fx button and point it to a DAX measure. The description then updates with slicers and data refresh.
- [ ] **Decorative elements are hidden from screen readers**  
  Background shapes, images and dividers add nothing for a screen reader. Hide them from the tab order in the Selection pane.
- [ ] **Alt text is short, clear and leads with the insight**  
  Aim for one or two sentences: what the chart shows, the key insight, then the current filter context, e.g. "Milestone forecast dates for Project P01. Build Complete has slipped 23 days across three schedule versions."
- [ ] **Dynamic alt text has been tested across different filter selections**  
  Change the slicers and read the alt text each time. Check the direction of every trend, how it handles blanks and single values, and that numbers and dates are formatted.
- [ ] **AI-generated DAX for alt text has been reviewed by a person**  
  AI can write the measure quickly, but the measure working doesn't mean the description is right.
- [ ] **Claude dashboards: every chart has a text summary or data table a screen reader can reach**  
  Ask Claude to add a visually hidden data table or a text summary under each chart, then check it with a screen reader.

#### T4 · Testing and maintenance

- [ ] **An automated accessibility check has been run**  
  Use [PBIX A11y](https://pbiaudits.com) for .pbix files, [pbir-a11y](https://github.com/Juls-BI/pbir-a11y) for PBIP projects, or the axe DevTools or WAVE browser extensions for web dashboards.
- [ ] **Automated checks are followed by manual testing**  
  Automated tools catch only some issues. Spend ten minutes on keyboard-only navigation and a quick screen reader pass with NVDA (free) or Narrator.
- [ ] **Checks run again whenever the report changes**  
  With PBIP, run `pbir-a11y check ./MyReport --fail-on warn` as a pre-commit hook or CI step, so new issues block the change.
- [ ] **Someone is responsible for accessibility after go-live**  
  Name an owner and add accessibility to the report's regular review, for example every quarter or with each major release.
- [ ] **Claude dashboards: checks are repeated after every regeneration**  
  Every time we ask Claude to change the dashboard, it may rewrite large parts of the page. Run axe or WAVE again, even for small changes.

### H: Humans

#### H1 · Users and context

- [ ] **We know who uses the report and what decisions they make**  
  Write one sentence per audience: "Project managers use this weekly to spot milestones at risk." If we can't, we need to talk to users first.
- [ ] **We know where and how they open it**  
  Ask whether they use a desktop, laptop, phone or shared screen in meetings. Design for the most common one, and test on the most difficult one.
- [ ] **AI was given the users' context, not just what to build**  
  Tell AI what people need to understand and decide, not only which chart to add. It can't know about access needs unless we tell it.

#### H2 · Accessibility needs

- [ ] **Accessibility needs were captured during requirements gathering**  
  Ask about access needs at the start, alongside other requirements. Accessibility isn't something we bolt onto the finished report.
- [ ] **We've considered different access needs**  
  Run through five quick personas: low vision, colour blindness, screen reader user, keyboard-only user and cognitive overload. What would each struggle with?
- [ ] **A report is the right format for everyone who needs it**  
  Sometimes a dashboard isn't the best way to get information to someone. Agree another accessible format or route where needed.
- [ ] **People with different access needs have given feedback**  
  Ask for volunteers through internal networks or accessibility groups, and make it easy to give feedback.
- [ ] **We've confirmed our fixes actually help**  
  After making changes, go back to the person who raised the issue. A fix that passes a checker might still not solve their problem.

#### H3 · Interaction and navigation

- [ ] **The whole report can be used with only a keyboard**  
  Put the mouse away. Use Ctrl + F6 to move into the report canvas, Tab to move between visuals and Esc to step back out.
- [ ] **Slicers, filters and buttons are clearly labelled**  
  Give each slicer a visible header and each button text or a tooltip. A plain icon with no label can be a mystery to screen reader users.
- [ ] **Keyboard focus is always visible**  
  As we tab through, we should always be able to see where we are. If the focus disappears behind a shape or image, check the layer order.
- [ ] **Claude dashboards: hover content also appears on keyboard focus**  
  Tab to each chart and filter. If a tooltip only appears on mouse hover, ask Claude to make it appear on focus too.
- [ ] **Tooltip information is also available somewhere else**  
  Tooltips need a mouse hover. If they hold essential information, add it to a data label, table or the alt text as well.
- [ ] **We've watched someone use the report with a keyboard or screen reader**  
  Watching is better than asking. Give them a real task and note where they hesitate, get lost or give up.

